\section{Fixed columns and Margin dimensions}{

\code{.by} marks fixed keys: columns present in every grouping set, which retain
their input types and never receive \code{.margin_label}. Every column
\code{.grouping} selects is a Margin dimension: one a grouping set can omit to
form a margin -- a subtotal or total row -- where it then holds the Margin
label. A grouped input's grouping columns are fixed keys, as \code{.by} columns
are.

\code{.by = year} with \code{.grouping = rollup(region, store)} produces the same
grouping sets as
\code{.grouping = grouping_spec(grouping_set(year), rollup(region, store))}, but
the two are not interchangeable: a column supplied through \code{.grouping} is a
Margin dimension even when every grouping set contains it, so it takes part
in \code{.margin_label} type conversion and collision checks. Use \code{.by} for
columns that must always remain fixed.

Both accept selection predicates through \code{\link[tidyselect:where]{tidyselect::where()}}. On a lazy
input, a predicate receives columns derived from the schema or a zero-row
proxy rather than the source values, so use one that depends only on the
column type, such as \code{where(is.character)}. A value-dependent predicate
such as \code{where(~ any(.x == "x"))} can silently select different columns on
a lazy input than on a local one; marginplyr does not detect it. To select
on values, call \code{\link[dplyr:collect]{dplyr::collect()}} first.
DuckDB, Arrow, and \code{dtplyr} inputs supply those types; SQLite, PostgreSQL,
and every other SQL connection cannot without a query, so a predicate is
refused there. Select the columns by name, or collect first.
}

\section{Grouped and row-wise inputs}{

When \code{.data} has been grouped with \code{\link[dplyr:group_by]{dplyr::group_by()}} and \code{.by} is \code{NULL},
its grouping columns become implicit fixed keys: \code{group_by(year)} followed
by \code{.grouping = rollup(region)} computes what \code{.by = year} does on the
ungrouped data.

As with \code{\link[dplyr:summarize]{dplyr::summarize()}} and \code{\link[tidyr:nest]{tidyr::nest()}}, a grouped input cannot
also supply \code{.by}; call \code{\link[dplyr:ungroup]{dplyr::ungroup()}} first when replacing the existing
groups. A grouping column also cannot appear in \code{.grouping}, because one
column cannot be both a fixed key and a dimension that can be rolled up.
Grouped local data created with \code{.drop = FALSE} is rejected because empty
groups cannot be preserved on every backend. To retain empty keys, complete
the input before the Margin operation, as \emph{Keep unused factor levels} in
\href{https://sayuks.github.io/marginplyr/vignettes/completing_keys.html#keep-unused-factor-levels}{Complete absent keys before margins} shows.

Row-wise input is rejected; to keep row-wise identifiers as fixed keys,
ungroup first and select them with \code{.by}. Each verb's page states the
grouping of its result.
}

\section{Display labels and grouping identity}{

\code{.margin_label} is a display value, not the identity of a grouping set;
each verb's Value states the type a dimension takes to hold a label. A
factor or ordered factor is rebuilt to hold a non-missing label, preserving
ordered status and placing the new level last by default or first when
\code{.margin_label_position = "first"}. A label equal to any declared level,
used or unused, is rejected before any grouping set is built, whatever
\code{.check_margin_label} says. Rebuilding preserves the distinction between an
observation that uses a factor NA level and an actually missing factor
code.

\code{NA_character_} and \code{NULL} both create a typed missing Margin value and no
factor level. What separates them is a factor NA level: \code{NA_character_} on
a factor that has one is refused, because NA is already a factor level,
even when \code{.check_margin_label = FALSE}, while \code{NULL} preserves the level.
Every other combination of label and factor is allowed.

A local packed data-frame, matrix, or array Margin dimension accepts only
\code{NULL} or \code{NA_character_} as its Margin label, because no scalar string can
represent such a key; split it into separate columns if it needs display
text.
The same holds for a list-column dimension, locally or under dtplyr, and
for an Arrow list, large list, or fixed-size list dimension. A DuckDB
\code{LIST} dimension takes a label: DuckDB converts it to text itself, in a
spelling that keeps distinct lists apart.

A factor observation that uses an NA level can print as \verb{<NA>} while
\code{is.na()} is false. A missing factor code has \code{is.na()} equal to true.
Source missing values and typed-missing Margin values may display
identically, so keep \code{.id} in the result when the difference matters.
}

\section{Margin order}{

\code{.sort} orders a result by a key built from its Grouping plan and its
values. The key is the result's own leading key columns, left
to right, with each Margin dimension preceded by its Grouping bit -- \code{1} on a
row whose grouping set omits that dimension, \code{0} otherwise -- and by
whether its value is missing:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{is.na(by1), by1, ..., bit(d1), is.na(d1), d1, bit(d2), is.na(d2), d2, ...
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A margin row therefore sits with the rows it summarizes instead of wherever
its \code{.margin_label} falls among that dimension's values: the label's text
never decides where a margin goes, and neither does the locale. Within a
level, rows sort ascending by value, with a source \code{NA} after the named
values. Fixed \code{.by} keys sort first, so each partition is one contiguous
block, and \code{.id}, when it names a column, breaks the remaining ties.
\code{"first"} reverses the Grouping bits alone: missing values come last
wherever they appear, on every backend.

Whether there is a label does decide how a non-factor dimension sorts: under
a non-missing \code{.margin_label} it is character, as each verb's Value says,
so a numeric dimension sorts as text, \code{"10"} before \code{"9"}. Keep its type
with \code{.margin_label = NULL}, or make it a factor first. Factor dimensions
sort by their levels, except on Arrow, which sorts a factor by value; see
\emph{Arrow inputs}.
\code{.margin_label_position} positions a synthetic factor level and never a
row.

A Margin order promises what \code{\link[dplyr:arrange]{dplyr::arrange()}} promises in the C locale,
which it fixes: \code{options(dplyr.legacy_locale = TRUE)} does not redirect it.
It is a property of the object the verb returns: the row order of a local
data frame, or the ordering of a lazy result that \code{\link[dplyr:collect]{dplyr::collect()}}
observes. Later verbs can change it. It composes with every \code{.duplicates}
policy the verb accepts.

\emph{Put each subtotal with the rows it summarizes} in
\href{https://sayuks.github.io/marginplyr/vignettes/recipes.html#put-each-subtotal-with-the-rows-it-summarizes}{Recipes for common reporting tasks} shows a Margin order before
and after, and shows the key written as an \code{arrange()} you control.
}

\section{Grouping set identifiers}{

When \code{.id} names an output column, each result row receives the one-based
position of its grouping set occurrence after applying \code{.duplicates}.
\code{"drop"} renumbers the occurrences it keeps. One grouping set has
identifier \code{1L}, and a zero-row result retains an integer \code{.id} column.

\code{.id} records plan occurrence, not physical result order, and is not a
durable business key: reordering or deduplicating the Grouping
specification changes it. \code{\link[=grouping_bit]{grouping_bit()}} documents how \code{.id} compares
with \code{\link[=inspect_grouping]{inspect_grouping()}}\verb{$set_id}, \code{\link[=grouping_bit]{grouping_bit()}}, and \code{\link[=grouping_id]{grouping_id()}}.

\code{\link[=grouping_bit]{grouping_bit()}} and \code{\link[=grouping_id]{grouping_id()}} can be called only inside
\code{\link[=summarize_with_margins]{summarize_with_margins()}}. To learn which grouping set a row of
\code{\link[=expand_with_margins]{expand_with_margins()}}, \code{\link[=nest_with_margins]{nest_with_margins()}}, or \code{\link[=nest_by_with_margins]{nest_by_with_margins()}}
came from, pass \code{.id} and join it to the \code{set_id} column of
\code{\link[=inspect_grouping]{inspect_grouping()}} called with the same \code{.by} and \code{.grouping}, as \emph{Add a
level column: detail, subtotal or total} in \href{https://sayuks.github.io/marginplyr/vignettes/recipes.html#add-a-level-column-detail-subtotal-or-total}{Recipes for common reporting tasks} does. Without \code{.id}, a typed-missing \code{.margin_label} leaves a
margin row indistinguishable from a source row whose dimension is missing, so
summarizing an expansion again by its grouping columns merges the two.
}

\section{Grouping plan size}{

A Grouping plan holding more than 4,096 grouping sets is refused with a
\code{"marginplyr_error"}, by every verb and on every backend, before any
grouping set is computed. Only a selection that needs a lazy input's column
types, such as \code{where(is.character)}, sends a query first: the metadata
reads \emph{When marginplyr queries your data} lists. The count is the plan's
after \code{.duplicates} is applied, the rows \code{\link[=inspect_grouping]{inspect_grouping()}} returns, so a
specification that writes many repeats of a few sets passes under
\code{.duplicates = "drop"}, except that a Cartesian product whose leading
factors alone pass the limit is refused even where a later factor would
merge their sets back within it. A \code{\link[=cube]{cube()}} of n dimensions is 2^n grouping
sets, so a \code{cube()} of 13 columns is refused; a selection such as
\code{cube(where(is.character))} reaches that on a wide table without naming a
column. Use fewer Margin dimensions or a narrower Grouping specification,
or set \code{options(marginplyr.max_grouping_sets = )} to a larger whole number,
or to \code{Inf} for no limit.

The limit keeps a plan from exhausting memory in your R session; it does
not make a large plan cheap, and a database's own limits, such as
PostgreSQL's refusal of more than 4,096 native grouping sets, still apply to
a plan the raised option admits. \emph{Grouping specifications from user input}
in \href{https://sayuks.github.io/marginplyr/vignettes/database_backends.html#grouping-specifications-from-user-input}{Database and lazy backends} says what a plan within the limit
still costs and what bounds an application's call.
}

\section{Option arguments}{

An argument documented as taking one of a listed set of strings —
\code{.duplicates}, \code{.margin_label_position}, and \code{.sort} on the Margin verbs,
and \code{.format} on \code{\link[=inspect_grouping]{inspect_grouping()}} — takes one of those strings, spelled
in full. An abbreviation is not shorthand for the value it begins, and
\code{NULL} is not shorthand for the default: both are errors naming the values
the argument accepts.

To ask for the default, leave the argument out, or pass the whole
vocabulary exactly as the signature spells it, which lets a function of your
own repeat \code{.sort = c("none", "last", "first")} and hand it on.
Any other vector of more than one value is an error, a reordering of the
vocabulary included. A wrapper that would rather not track the signature
resolves the value itself and passes one string.
}

\section{Result metadata}{

The result's class, its dataset-wide attributes, and the attributes of
each column follow the native dplyr or tidyr operation the verb
corresponds to on the same backend, applied per grouping set and combined
in Grouping-plan order. marginplyr adds the following:
\itemize{
\item A source column's variable label (\code{attr(x, "label")}) is kept wherever
that column appears in the result, including on zero rows. Calculated
summaries and generated columns such as \code{.id} gain no label.
\item Factor and ordered-factor dimensions keep their levels and ordered
status; see \emph{Display labels and grouping identity}. Classed columns such
as \link{Date} and \link{POSIXct}, including its \code{tzone}, are carried unchanged.
\item A dimension's margin rows hold the label, or a typed missing value,
without element names; its other rows keep the names the native
operation keeps.
}

These guarantees cover the returned result and direct \code{\link[dplyr:collect]{dplyr::collect()}}.
Later verbs and \code{\link[dplyr:compute]{dplyr::compute()}} follow native backend rules. A lazy
result uses the input schema captured when it was built, so rebuild it
after changing column types, factor definitions, or labels.
}

\section{Arrow inputs}{

Arrow's tabular dplyr inputs are a \code{Table}, \code{RecordBatch}, \code{Dataset},
\code{RecordBatchReader}, or an \code{arrow_dplyr_query} built from them. A summary or
expansion of one stays an Arrow query, except that a \code{RecordBatchReader}, and
a query whose source graph contains one, is refused: a reader is one-pass,
while a Margin operation may build one branch per grouping set, so
\code{\link[arrow:as_arrow_table]{arrow::as_arrow_table()}} has to materialize the reusable \code{Table} first. The
nesting verbs refuse every Arrow input; collect it first. Other Arrow
objects, such as a \code{Scanner} or an \code{Array}, are not dplyr inputs; a Scanner
materializes with \verb{$ToTable()}. \emph{Which Arrow inputs are supported} in
\href{https://sayuks.github.io/marginplyr/vignettes/database_backends.html#which-arrow-inputs-are-supported}{Database and lazy backends} tabulates these answers per verb.

For a summary or generated Margin label transformation Arrow's own engine
cannot evaluate, a \code{Table} and \code{RecordBatch} (and queries built on them)
would fall back to R by reading every column of the input, not only the
ones the expression names. marginplyr refuses such an expression instead,
before a row is read, and names the two rewrites that compute it: collect
the input first, and select the columns the summary needs before
collecting, which is the narrowing Arrow's own route cannot do for you. A
\code{Dataset} and a query built on one never read themselves into R, so they
keep Arrow's own unsupported-expression error instead.

Which expressions those are is Arrow's to decide and moves with its version,
so they are not listed here. Among the summaries refused are a group
collapsed into a single value, such as pasting one, a subset written inside
an aggregate, and a statistic over two columns at once; ordinary numeric
summaries, and arithmetic over them, are evaluated by Arrow and stay lazy.

Arrow does not hold factor levels in the schema marginplyr reads, so a
non-missing \code{.margin_label} converts a factor dimension to character, and its
levels and ordered status are lost. A Margin order sorts a factor by value on
Arrow whatever the label, fixed \code{.by} keys included, even where the result
column keeps its levels.
For the same reason, a label equal to an unused declared level is allowed,
and an observed collision is refused only when
\code{.check_margin_label = TRUE}. \code{NA_character_} and \code{NULL} convert nothing.
}

\section{Database backend coverage}{

Most summaries on DuckDB and PostgreSQL use one native
\verb{GROUP BY GROUPING SETS} query. Other database backends, every expansion,
and the summaries listed under \emph{Portable fallback SQL} in
\href{https://sayuks.github.io/marginplyr/vignettes/database_backends.html#portable-fallback-sql}{Database and lazy backends} use a portable \verb{UNION ALL} query with one
branch per grouping set and the same result. Asking for a Margin order never
changes which of the two runs. The text of a converted dimension is each
backend's own cast, so it can differ from a local result's; \emph{Converted
dimension text} in that guide tabulates it. The \emph{Backend support matrix}
there sets each backend family's answers side by side, including what
becomes of a factor dimension.

Continuous integration executes Margin queries on these backends:
\itemize{
\item DuckDB, SQLite, Arrow, and \code{dtplyr}: the test suite.
\item PostgreSQL, on a live server: Parent and Total shares, including inside
a transaction the caller holds, typed missing dimensions, the observed
label check, expansion input, and window-ordered input. The rest of its
coverage is SQL generation.
\item Microsoft SQL Server, on a live server: portable summaries, compared with
the local result. Its expansion coverage is SQL generation.
}

SQL generation, not execution, is verified for Access, SAP HANA, Hive,
Impala, MariaDB, MySQL, Oracle, Amazon Redshift, Snowflake, Spark SQL, and
Teradata, and for generic DBI and ODBC connections.

On SQLite and DuckDB, identifiers differing only in ASCII letter case name
the same column, so a result refuses two public columns that would collide
under that rule and names both spellings.

A lazy result's Margin order is the outermost query's \verb{ORDER BY}, which
\code{\link[dplyr:collect]{dplyr::collect()}} and \code{\link[dplyr:compute]{dplyr::compute()}} observe. Whether it survives
later verbs depends on dbplyr's query flattening and is not promised. It is
not a window ordering, so a window function over a sorted result still
needs \code{\link[dbplyr:window_order]{dbplyr::window_order()}}. For direct \code{\link[dplyr:compute]{dplyr::compute()}} on SQLite,
see \emph{\link[=summarize_with_margins]{SQLite materialization}} in
\code{\link[=summarize_with_margins]{summarize_with_margins()}}.
}

\section{When marginplyr queries your data}{

A summary or expansion of a lazy input builds a query and returns it
unexecuted. \code{\link[dplyr:show_query]{dplyr::show_query()}} runs nothing, and no row is read until
you execute the query yourself. The nesting verbs' pages state what each
does with a \code{dtplyr} step.

Building it can still send internal queries that read no source values:
\itemize{
\item \strong{A zero-row read of the input}, sent only to a backend whose factor
columns marginplyr must decompose and later restore -- currently
\code{dtplyr} and DuckDB -- to recover their levels and column prototypes. It
references your table but reads none of it, as the zero-row read
\code{\link[dplyr:tbl]{dplyr::tbl()}} already sends does. A derived \code{dtplyr} step whose types
cannot be found that way is refused; call \code{\link[dplyr:collect]{dplyr::collect()}} on it
explicitly and pass the data frame.
\item \strong{One DuckDB catalog query per operation at most}, reading table and
view names, stored view definitions, macro names, and the search path, to
check that the input hides no source work behind a view or macro before
its types are read. It binds no table of yours. Table macros, macros you
define, \code{query()}, \code{query_table()}, and views without stored SQL are
refused, except a data frame registered with \code{duckdb::duckdb_register()}.
Other table functions, such as \code{read_parquet()} or \code{range()}, are
admitted inside a view as they are when written directly.
A macro you define is refused however the query reaches it: written in
SQL, or called from a dplyr verb such as \code{mutate(y = my_macro(x))},
including a function dbplyr translates to that macro's name. Call
\code{\link[dplyr:compute]{dplyr::compute()}} or \code{\link[dplyr:collect]{dplyr::collect()}} first to use one.
DuckDB's own scalar macros, such as \code{nullif()}, are admitted only while
the database holds no macro you defined, since one could replace a name
inside their bodies.
\item \strong{At most two queries per share request until the SQL dialect answers},
for a summary that requests a share, referencing none of your tables;
see \emph{Parent shares use a staged lazy query} in
\href{https://sayuks.github.io/marginplyr/vignettes/database_backends.html#parent-shares-use-a-staged-lazy-query}{Database and lazy backends}.
}

A query is not safe merely because it returns no rows: some services bill
by the bytes a query scans, and \verb{LIMIT 0} is not exempt. marginplyr cannot
tell a billed connection from a free one, so \code{.check_margin_label} defaults
to \code{is.data.frame(.data)} and a lazy input is checked only when asked;
\emph{\link[=nest_by_with_margins]{dtplyr inputs}} in \code{\link[=nest_by_with_margins]{nest_by_with_margins()}} states
the one exception. The observed check scans every input row of the checked
columns and retrieves one row of collision counts.

With recording enabled before the call, \code{\link[=last_sent_queries]{last_sent_queries()}} lists the
SQL prepared for these internal queries in the order sent.
}

